# Conformance test wishlist
# These are scenarios we want to eventually have as passing tests.
# Runtime tests may cover many of these; this is the backlog.

# --- Line continuation / multi-line ---
# - Multi-line array literal with trailing commas
# - Multi-line map literal with trailing commas
# - Multi-line invocation args (named and shorthand)
# - Binary expression continued after operator (a +\n    b)
# - Chained dot access across lines (foo.\n    bar.\n    baz)
# - Chained invocations across lines (foo(x).\n    bar(y))
# - Parenthesized expression spanning lines
# - If condition spanning multiple lines
# - For-in invocation with multi-line args
# - Nested multi-line literals (array of maps, map of arrays)

# --- Expressions ---
# - Invocation with type args: foo[t = int](x)
# - Invocation with shorthand type args: foo[int](x)
# - Deeply nested binary expressions with mixed precedence
# - All binary operators at each precedence level
# - Prefix unary chaining (!!a, -(-x))
# - Nil narrowing: expr?!, expr? else! val
# - As narrowing: expr as! type, expr as type else! val
# - Postfix early-return ! on various expressions
# - Nil coalescing ?? with chained access
# - outer access in nested scopes
# - Parenthesized subexpressions
# - String literals with all escape sequences
# - Raw strings (now N-backtick delimited, N >= 2)
# - Number literals with underscores (1_000_000)
# - Float literals (with/without leading zero)

# --- Anonymous expressions ---
# - Anonymous data: data { name: str = "hello" \n age = 42 }
# - Anonymous data empty: data {}
# - Anonymous func: func { in x: int \n var y = x + 1 }
# - Anonymous view func: view func { in x: int \n x + 1 }
# - Anonymous noblock func: noblock func { in x: int \n x + 1 }
# - Anonymous data with member funcs (named data only, not anonymous)

# --- Statements ---
# - var with type, with expression, with both, shorthand :=
# - All assignment operators (=, +=, -=, *=, /=, %=)
# - if / else if / else chains
# - if narrowing-as: if x := val as int { }
# - if narrowing-as multi: if a, b := x as int, y as str { }
# - if narrowing-nil: if x := x? { }
# - if narrowing-nil multi: if a, b := x?, y? { }
# - else if with narrowing form independent of if form
# - Nested if inside if
# - for infinite, condition, for-in
# - break and continue
# - defer with multi-statement block
# - implicitly block form and rest-of-block form
# - implicitly with multiple expressions
# - return and return!
# - Standalone block { } creating scope

# --- Types ---
# - Named type: simple (int), qualified (pkg.type)
# - Nilable: int?
# - Entity ref: &int, &pkg.type, &int?
# - Generics: list[int], map[key = str, value = int]
# - Generic shorthand: list[int] (name = value derived from ident)
# - Trailing comma in type args: list[int,]
# - Type introspection: name@type, name@typein, name@typeout
# - Anonymous data: data { :int }
# - Anonymous func: func { :int }, view func { :int }, noblock func { :int }
# - Anonymous entity: entity { :int }
# - Anonymous type field modifiers: in, out, out!, inout
# - Anonymous type field with default: :int = ???
# - Anonymous type field shorthand name derivation: :some_type -> name "some_type"
# - Shorthand field name derivation from entity ref: :&int -> name "int"
# - Shorthand field name derivation from generic: :list[int] -> name "list"
# - Data with shorthand fields (simple, entity ref, generic, nilable)

# --- Constructs ---
# - Data with fields only
# - Data with fields, init, run, functions
# - Entity with fields and functions
# - Function with parameters and return type
# - Nested data inside data
# - Multiple annotations on a construct
# - Type alias

# --- Source-level ---
# - Imports (single, multiple, aliased)
# - Module-level annotations
# - Multiple constructs in one file
# - Blank line enforcement between constructs
# - Line length: 120-char exempt when no space after indent/comment prefix (URLs, long qualified names)
